  "query_preview": "..Conditions remain relatively benign through\nSaturday, with the main threat through this period being gusty\nsouthwest winds across the region. Winds remain elevated through\nthis evening with widespread gusts 30-45 mph, before tapering\nmainly across lower elevations overnight. A strong low-level jet\nmoving overhead Friday morning will result in similarly gusty \nwinds quickly redeveloping across much of Utah and southwest \nWyoming by late morning, continuing through the evening. Have\nopted to hold off from headlines despite some areas likely hitting\nadvisory criteria especially Friday, given the lack of spatial\ncoverage of winds in excess of 45 mph and the fact the low-level\njet maximizes in the morning instead of peak mixing in the\nafternoon. The deep trough off the coast of California driving \nthese winds retrogrades slightly westward off the coast on \nSaturday, easing upper level winds and resulting in lighter, \nthough still breezy, winds on Saturday afternoon. \n\nIncreasing moisture is expected across the region through the\nweekend, though chances for showers and thunderstorms remains low\nthrough Saturday as we will lack an outside forcing. Conditions\nshift drastically by Sunday. The aforementioned trough along the \nCalifornia coast is expected to eject eastward through northern \nUtah on Sunday, providing ample amounts of instability; \nconcurrently, deep tropical moisture associated with what is now \nHurricane Marie advects northward into the region as it moves \nnorthwest along the Baja Peninsula. This will result in widespread\nshowers and thunderstorms developing across much of Utah and\nsouthwest Wyoming on Sunday. Given the deep moisture that will be\nin place, heavy rain will be a threat across much of the region,\nwith main concerns for flash flooding across central and southern\nUtah where soils are already primed from recent rainfall. Across\nnorthern Utah, the potential for severe thunderstorms is beginning\nto emerge -- forecasted environmental conditions are becoming\nincreasingly favorable given the passage of the aforementioned\ntrough overhead. Plentiful instability and shear will exist,\nsupportive of more robust updrafts capable of producing wet\nmicroburst winds, hail, and lightning.\n\nAfter the passage of the trough on Sunday, broad ridging under dry\nsouthwest flow quickly allows any residual convective activity to\nwane early Monday. Dry conditions look to prevail through at least\nmid-next week, with overall pleasantly cooler seasonable\ntemperatures also expected through this period.",
